{
  "dom_id": "step-q14506",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "A triangle has sides $j$, $k$, and $l.$ The sides $j$ and $l$ have lengths $4$ and $7$, respectively. If the angle $K$ between $j$ and $l$ has a measure of ${121}^{∘}$, what is the length of side $k?$ Round your answer to two decimal places.",
  "worked_solution": "The following triangle illustrates the information given.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14252321/assets/1fde3029f6a50388ebd342dc4d15e9787567461b651c24cd6bd6843521b0465d.png)\n\nThe Law of Cosines states that\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}={j}^{2}+{l}^{2}-2jl\\operatorname{cos}⁡K.\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nm∠K={121}^{∘},\\,j=4,\\,l=7.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the numbers into the formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2} & ={4}^{2}+{7}^{2}-2(4)(7)\\operatorname{cos}⁡{121}^{∘} \\\\  & =16+49-56\\operatorname{cos}⁡{121}^{∘} \\\\  & =65-56(-0.515\\,03…) \\\\  & =65+28.842\\,13 \\\\  & =93.842\\,13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places. \n\nTaking the square root gives\n\n$$\nk=\\sqrt{93.842\\,13}=9.69\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
